\section{Model}

We study a two-country sovereign default economy with contagion through common, risk-averse international lenders. The world economy consists of two borrowing countries, denoted $O$ and $P$, and a continuum of identical foreign investors. Time is discrete and infinite.

The recursive structure follows the leader-follower formulation in the spirit of de Ferra and Mallucci. When both countries have market access, country $O$ acts first and country $P$ acts after observing $O$'s current policy. When only one country has market access, only that country makes meaningful debt-market decisions. Our departure from the endowment setup is that each country is instead a production economy with endogenous labor supply and a government that finances public goods through a distortionary tax on consumption. Accordingly, the sovereign's policy problem is naturally written in terms of debt issuance and taxation, with public expenditure pinned down residually by the government budget constraint.

\subsection{Environment}

Each country $i\in\{O,P\}$ issues one-period, non-contingent debt. Let $b_i\ge 0$ denote the stock of debt due in the current period, and let $b_i'\ge 0$ denote new debt issued in the current period and due next period.

At the beginning of each period, country $i$ either has access to international financial markets or is temporarily excluded from them. We summarize this status by an indicator $x_i\in\{0,1\}$, where $x_i=0$ denotes market access and $x_i=1$ denotes exclusion. Let
\[
s=(y,b,x)
\]
denote the aggregate state, where $y=(y_O,y_P)$ is the vector of country-specific productivity shifters, $b=(b_O,b_P)$ is the vector of debt due, and $x=(x_O,x_P)$ is the vector of access indicators. We restrict attention to admissible states satisfying
\[
x_i=1 \Rightarrow b_i=0.
\]

The vector $y$ follows a Markov process, potentially correlated across countries.

\subsection{Production, households, and taxation}

Country $i$ produces output according to
\[
Y_i = A_i y_i l_i,
\]
where $A_i>0$ is a scale parameter and $l_i\in[0,1]$ is labor supply.

The representative household in country $i$ has preferences
\[
\E_0 \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}\beta^t \left[ u(c_{i,t},1-l_{i,t}) + v(g_{i,t}) \right],
\]
where $c_i$ denotes private consumption and $g_i$ denotes the public good.

The government levies a proportional tax on consumption, $\tau_i\ge 0$. The household budget constraint is
\begin{equation}
(1+\tau_i)c_i = A_i \tilde y_i l_i,
\end{equation}
where effective productivity is
\[
\tilde y_i =
\begin{cases}
y_i, & \text{if } x_i=0 \text{ and the sovereign repays in the current period},\\[4pt]
h_i(y_i), & \text{if } x_i=1 \text{ or the sovereign defaults in the current period},
\end{cases}
\qquad 0<h_i(y_i)\le y_i.
\]

Household optimality implies the intratemporal condition
\begin{equation}
\frac{u_{1-l}(c_i,1-l_i)}{u_c(c_i,1-l_i)}
=
\frac{A_i\tilde y_i}{1+\tau_i}.
\end{equation}
For every admissible pair $(\tau_i,\tilde y_i)$, the household budget constraint and intratemporal first-order condition jointly determine private allocations $(c_i^*,l_i^*)$.

Because the public good is tax financed, it is not treated as an independent control. Once the government chooses its tax rate and, in repayment states, its issuance decision, public expenditure is pinned down residually by the government budget constraint.

\subsection{Government budget constraints and resource feasibility}

If country $i$ has market access and repays in the current period, the government budget constraint is
\begin{equation}
g_i = \tau_i c_i^* + q_i(\cdot)b_i' - b_i.
\end{equation}
Equivalently,
\begin{equation}
g_i + b_i = \tau_i c_i^* + q_i(\cdot)b_i'.
\end{equation}

If instead the government defaults or is excluded, it cannot issue new debt, and
\begin{equation}
g_i = \tau_i c_i^*.
\end{equation}

Combining the household budget constraint with the government budget constraint yields the consolidated country resource constraints. In repayment states,
\begin{equation}
c_i^* + g_i = A_i y_i l_i^* - b_i + q_i(\cdot)b_i'.
\end{equation}
In default or exclusion states,
\begin{equation}
c_i^* + g_i = A_i h_i(y_i) l_i^*.
\end{equation}

\subsection{Default, exclusion, and readmission}

If country $i$ has market access, it chooses whether to repay or default. Let $d_i\in\{0,1\}$, where $d_i=1$ denotes default. If $x_i=1$, the country is already excluded and makes no meaningful debt-market choice, although it still chooses a tax rate and thereby determines public expenditure.

Debt issuance is constrained by repayment and market access:
\begin{equation}
b_i' = 0 \qquad \text{if } x_i=1 \text{ or } d_i=1.
\end{equation}

Exclusion is temporary. Let $\lambda_i\in(0,1)$ denote the probability that country $i$ regains market access in the next period, conditional on being excluded at the end of the current period. Debt evolves according to
\begin{equation}
b_{i,t+1}=
\begin{cases}
b_{i,t}', & \text{if } x_{i,t}=0 \text{ and } d_{i,t}=0,\\[4pt]
0, & \text{if } x_{i,t}=1 \text{ or } d_{i,t}=1.
\end{cases}
\end{equation}

\subsection{International lenders and bond pricing}

International lenders are risk averse and price sovereign bonds using their intertemporal marginal rate of substitution. Whenever country $i$'s debt is traded,
\begin{equation}
q_i(\cdot)=
\beta_L
\E\left[
\frac{U_L'(C_L')}{U_L'(C_L)}(1-d_i(s'))
\right].
\end{equation}
When only one country has market access, the corresponding price schedule is denoted $q_i^S(\cdot)$.

\subsection{Timing of the game}

Within each period, productivity and readmission shocks are realized. If both countries have market access, country $O$ first chooses a current policy vector
\[
a_O=(d_O,b_O',\tau_O),
\]
subject to $b_O'=0$ if $d_O=1$. Country $P$ then observes $a_O$ and chooses
\[
a_P=(d_P,b_P',\tau_P),
\]
subject to $b_P'=0$ if $d_P=1$. Households then choose private allocations, current obligations are settled, new debt is traded, and the economy transitions to the next period.

\section{Recursive sovereign problems}


\subsection{Both countries have market access}

Consider the regime $(x_O,x_P)=(0,0)$.

\subsubsection{Periphery's problem}

Given the leader's current policy vector
\[
a_O=(d_O,b_O',\tau_O),
\]
the follower's conditional value is
\begin{equation}
\widehat V_P(s;a_O)
=
\max\left\{
\sup_{(b_P',\tau_P)\in \Gamma_P^{r}(s;a_O)} W_P^{r}(s;a_O,b_P',\tau_P),
\;
\sup_{\tau_P\in \Gamma_P^{d}(s;a_O)} W_P^{d}(s;a_O,\tau_P)
\right\}.
\end{equation}

\paragraph{Repayment branch.}
For any observed $a_O$ and any repayment choice $(b_P',\tau_P)$, define
\begin{equation}
W_P^{r}(s;a_O,b_P',\tau_P)
=
u(c_P^*,1-l_P^*)+v(g_P)
+\beta\E\!\left[\mathcal C_P^{r}(s';a_O,b_P',\tau_P)\right],
\end{equation}
subject to
\begin{align}
(1+\tau_P)c_P^* &= A_P y_P l_P^*,\\
\frac{u_{1-l}(c_P^*,1-l_P^*)}{u_c(c_P^*,1-l_P^*)}
&=
\frac{A_P y_P}{1+\tau_P},\\
g_P &= \tau_P c_P^* + q_P(s;a_O,b_P',\tau_P)b_P' - b_P,\\
c_P^* + g_P &= A_P y_P l_P^* - b_P + q_P(s;a_O,b_P',\tau_P)b_P',
\end{align}
with
\[
b_P'\ge 0,\qquad \tau_P\ge 0,\qquad g_P\ge 0.
\]

The continuation value is
\begin{equation}
\mathcal C_P^{r}(s';a_O,b_P',\tau_P)
=
(1-d_O)V_P^{00}(s')
+
d_O\Big[(1-\lambda_O)V_P^{10}(s')+\lambda_O V_P^{00}(s')\Big].
\end{equation}

The feasible set is
\[
\Gamma_P^{r}(s;a_O)
=
\left\{
(b_P',\tau_P):
b_P'\ge 0,\ \tau_P\ge 0,\ g_P(s;a_O,b_P',\tau_P)\ge 0
\right\}.
\]

\paragraph{Default branch.}
For any observed $a_O$ and any default-state tax choice $\tau_P$, define
\begin{equation}
W_P^{d}(s;a_O,\tau_P)
=
u(c_P^*,1-l_P^*)+v(g_P)
+\beta\E\!\left[\mathcal C_P^{d}(s';a_O,\tau_P)\right],
\end{equation}
subject to
\begin{align}
(1+\tau_P)c_P^* &= A_P h_P(y_P)l_P^*,\\
\frac{u_{1-l}(c_P^*,1-l_P^*)}{u_c(c_P^*,1-l_P^*)}
&=
\frac{A_P h_P(y_P)}{1+\tau_P},\\
g_P &= \tau_P c_P^*,\\
c_P^* + g_P &= A_P h_P(y_P)l_P^*,
\end{align}
with
\[
\tau_P\ge 0,\qquad g_P\ge 0.
\]

Its continuation value is
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\mathcal C_P^{d}(s';a_O,\tau_P)
=&(1-d_O)\Big[(1-\lambda_P)V_P^{01}(s')+\lambda_PV_P^{00}(s')\Big]\\
&+d_O\Big[
(1-\lambda_P)(1-\lambda_O)V_P^{11}(s')
+(1-\lambda_P)\lambda_O V_P^{01}(s')\\
&\qquad\qquad+\lambda_P(1-\lambda_O)V_P^{10}(s')
+\lambda_P\lambda_O V_P^{00}(s')
\Big].
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}

The feasible set is
\[
\Gamma_P^{d}(s;a_O)=\{\tau_P:\tau_P\ge 0\}.
\]

The follower's default rule is therefore
\begin{equation}
\hat d_P(s;a_O)
=
\1\left\{
\sup_{\tau_P\in\Gamma_P^{d}(s;a_O)}W_P^{d}(s;a_O,\tau_P)
\ge
\sup_{(b_P',\tau_P)\in\Gamma_P^{r}(s;a_O)}W_P^{r}(s;a_O,b_P',\tau_P)
\right\},
\end{equation}
and its repayment policy rule is
\begin{equation}
(\hat b_P',\hat\tau_P)(s;a_O)
\in
\argmax_{(b_P',\tau_P)\in\Gamma_P^{r}(s;a_O)}
W_P^{r}(s;a_O,b_P',\tau_P),
\end{equation}
with $\hat b_P'(s;a_O)=0$ whenever $\hat d_P(s;a_O)=1$.

\subsubsection{Outskirt's problem}

The leader's equilibrium value in the joint-access regime is
\begin{equation}
V_O^{00}(s)
=
\max\left\{
\sup_{(b_O',\tau_O)\in\Gamma_O^{r}(s)}W_O^{r}(s;b_O',\tau_O),
\;
\sup_{\tau_O\in\Gamma_O^{d}(s)}W_O^{d}(s;\tau_O)
\right\}.
\end{equation}

\paragraph{Repayment branch.}
For any repayment choice $(b_O',\tau_O)$, define
\begin{equation}
W_O^{r}(s;b_O',\tau_O)
=
u(c_O^*,1-l_O^*)+v(g_O)
+\beta\E\!\left[\mathcal C_O^{r}(s';b_O',\tau_O)\right],
\end{equation}
subject to
\begin{align}
(1+\tau_O)c_O^* &= A_O y_O l_O^*,\\
\frac{u_{1-l}(c_O^*,1-l_O^*)}{u_c(c_O^*,1-l_O^*)}
&=
\frac{A_O y_O}{1+\tau_O},\\
g_O &= \tau_O c_O^* + q_O(s;a_O,\hat a_P(s;a_O))b_O' - b_O,\\
c_O^* + g_O
&=
A_O y_O l_O^*
-b_O
+
q_O(s;a_O,\hat a_P(s;a_O))b_O',
\end{align}
with
\[
b_O'\ge 0,\qquad \tau_O\ge 0,\qquad g_O\ge 0,
\]
where $a_O=(0,b_O',\tau_O)$.

Its continuation value is
\begin{equation}
\mathcal C_O^{r}(s';b_O',\tau_O)
=
(1-\hat d_P(s;a_O))V_O^{00}(s')
+
\hat d_P(s;a_O)\Big[(1-\lambda_P)V_O^{01}(s')+\lambda_PV_O^{00}(s')\Big].
\end{equation}

The feasible set is
\[
\Gamma_O^{r}(s)
=
\left\{
(b_O',\tau_O):
b_O'\ge 0,\ \tau_O\ge 0,\ g_O(s;b_O',\tau_O)\ge 0
\right\}.
\]

\paragraph{Default branch.}
For any default-state tax choice $\tau_O$, define
\begin{equation}
W_O^{d}(s;\tau_O)
=
u(c_O^*,1-l_O^*)+v(g_O)
+\beta\E\!\left[\mathcal C_O^{d}(s';\tau_O)\right],
\end{equation}
subject to
\begin{align}
(1+\tau_O)c_O^* &= A_O h_O(y_O)l_O^*,\\
\frac{u_{1-l}(c_O^*,1-l_O^*)}{u_c(c_O^*,1-l_O^*)}
&=
\frac{A_O h_O(y_O)}{1+\tau_O},\\
g_O &= \tau_O c_O^*,\\
c_O^* + g_O &= A_O h_O(y_O)l_O^*,
\end{align}
with
\[
\tau_O\ge 0,\qquad g_O\ge 0.
\]

Its continuation value is
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\mathcal C_O^{d}(s';\tau_O)
=&(1-\hat d_P(s;a_O^d))\Big[(1-\lambda_O)V_O^{10}(s')+\lambda_OV_O^{00}(s')\Big]\\
&+\hat d_P(s;a_O^d)\Big[
(1-\lambda_O)(1-\lambda_P)V_O^{11}(s')
+(1-\lambda_O)\lambda_PV_O^{10}(s')\\
&\qquad\qquad+\lambda_O(1-\lambda_P)V_O^{01}(s')
+\lambda_O\lambda_PV_O^{00}(s')
\Big],
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
where $a_O^d=(1,0,\tau_O)$.

The feasible set is
\[
\Gamma_O^{d}(s)=\{\tau_O:\tau_O\ge 0\}.
\]

Once the leader's equilibrium policy $a_O^*(s)$ is determined, the follower's conditional objects become state-only equilibrium objects:
\begin{align}
d_P(s) &= \hat d_P(s;a_O^*(s)),\\
b_P'(s) &= \hat b_P'(s;a_O^*(s)),\\
\tau_P(s) &= \hat\tau_P(s;a_O^*(s)),
\end{align}
and
\begin{equation}
V_P^{00}(s)=\widehat V_P(s;a_O^*(s)).
\end{equation}

\subsection{Only one country has market access}

We now turn to the case in which only one country has market access.

\subsubsection{Only $O$ has access: $(x_O,x_P)=(0,1)$}

The equilibrium value is
\begin{equation}
V_O^{01}(s)
=
\max\left\{
\sup_{(b_O',\tau_O)\in\Gamma_O^{r,01}(s)}W_O^{r,01}(s;b_O',\tau_O),
\;
\sup_{\tau_O\in\Gamma_O^{d,01}(s)}W_O^{d,01}(s;\tau_O)
\right\}.
\end{equation}

For any repayment choice $(b_O',\tau_O)$,
\begin{equation}
W_O^{r,01}(s;b_O',\tau_O)
=
u(c_O^*,1-l_O^*)+v(g_O)+\beta\E[\mathcal C_O^{r,01}(s')],
\end{equation}
subject to
\begin{align}
(1+\tau_O)c_O^* &= A_O y_O l_O^*,\\
\frac{u_{1-l}(c_O^*,1-l_O^*)}{u_c(c_O^*,1-l_O^*)}
&=
\frac{A_O y_O}{1+\tau_O},\\
g_O &= \tau_O c_O^* + q_O^S(s;b_O',\tau_O)b_O' - b_O,\\
c_O^* + g_O &= A_O y_O l_O^* - b_O + q_O^S(s;b_O',\tau_O)b_O',
\end{align}
with
\[
b_O'\ge 0,\qquad \tau_O\ge 0,\qquad g_O\ge 0,
\]
and continuation
\begin{equation}
\mathcal C_O^{r,01}(s')
=
(1-\lambda_P)V_O^{01}(s')+\lambda_PV_O^{00}(s').
\end{equation}

For any default-state tax choice $\tau_O$,
\begin{equation}
W_O^{d,01}(s;\tau_O)
=
u(c_O^*,1-l_O^*)+v(g_O)+\beta\E[\mathcal C_O^{d,01}(s')],
\end{equation}
subject to
\begin{align}
(1+\tau_O)c_O^* &= A_O h_O(y_O)l_O^*,\\
\frac{u_{1-l}(c_O^*,1-l_O^*)}{u_c(c_O^*,1-l_O^*)}
&=
\frac{A_O h_O(y_O)}{1+\tau_O},\\
g_O &= \tau_O c_O^*,\\
c_O^* + g_O &= A_O h_O(y_O)l_O^*,
\end{align}
with
\[
\tau_O\ge 0,\qquad g_O\ge 0,
\]
and continuation
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\mathcal C_O^{d,01}(s')
=&(1-\lambda_O)(1-\lambda_P)V_O^{11}(s')
+(1-\lambda_O)\lambda_PV_O^{10}(s')\\
&+\lambda_O(1-\lambda_P)V_O^{01}(s')
+\lambda_O\lambda_PV_O^{00}(s').
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}

\subsubsection{Only $P$ has access: $(x_O,x_P)=(1,0)$}

Symmetrically,
\begin{equation}
V_P^{10}(s)
=
\max\left\{
\sup_{(b_P',\tau_P)\in\Gamma_P^{r,10}(s)}W_P^{r,10}(s;b_P',\tau_P),
\;
\sup_{\tau_P\in\Gamma_P^{d,10}(s)}W_P^{d,10}(s;\tau_P)
\right\}.
\end{equation}

For any repayment choice $(b_P',\tau_P)$,
\begin{equation}
W_P^{r,10}(s;b_P',\tau_P)
=
u(c_P^*,1-l_P^*)+v(g_P)+\beta\E[\mathcal C_P^{r,10}(s')],
\end{equation}
subject to
\begin{align}
(1+\tau_P)c_P^* &= A_P y_P l_P^*,\\
\frac{u_{1-l}(c_P^*,1-l_P^*)}{u_c(c_P^*,1-l_P^*)}
&=
\frac{A_P y_P}{1+\tau_P},\\
g_P &= \tau_P c_P^* + q_P^S(s;b_P',\tau_P)b_P' - b_P,\\
c_P^* + g_P &= A_P y_P l_P^* - b_P + q_P^S(s;b_P',\tau_P)b_P',
\end{align}
with
\[
b_P'\ge 0,\qquad \tau_P\ge 0,\qquad g_P\ge 0,
\]
and continuation
\begin{equation}
\mathcal C_P^{r,10}(s')
=
(1-\lambda_O)V_P^{10}(s')+\lambda_OV_P^{00}(s').
\end{equation}

For any default-state tax choice $\tau_P$,
\begin{equation}
W_P^{d,10}(s;\tau_P)
=
u(c_P^*,1-l_P^*)+v(g_P)+\beta\E[\mathcal C_P^{d,10}(s')],
\end{equation}
subject to
\begin{align}
(1+\tau_P)c_P^* &= A_P h_P(y_P)l_P^*,\\
\frac{u_{1-l}(c_P^*,1-l_P^*)}{u_c(c_P^*,1-l_P^*)}
&=
\frac{A_P h_P(y_P)}{1+\tau_P},\\
g_P &= \tau_P c_P^*,\\
c_P^* + g_P &= A_P h_P(y_P)l_P^*,
\end{align}
with
\[
\tau_P\ge 0,\qquad g_P\ge 0,
\]
and continuation
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\mathcal C_P^{d,10}(s')
=&(1-\lambda_P)(1-\lambda_O)V_P^{11}(s')
+(1-\lambda_P)\lambda_OV_P^{01}(s')\\
&+\lambda_P(1-\lambda_O)V_P^{10}(s')
+\lambda_P\lambda_OV_P^{00}(s').
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}

\subsection{Both countries excluded: $(x_O,x_P)=(1,1)$}

When both countries are excluded, neither can issue debt. Each country chooses only its tax rate. For country $i\in\{O,P\}$,
\begin{equation}
V_i^{11}(s)
=
\sup_{\tau_i\in\Gamma_i^{11}(s)}W_i^{11}(s;\tau_i),
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation}
W_i^{11}(s;\tau_i)
=
u(c_i^*,1-l_i^*)+v(g_i)+\beta\E[\mathcal C_i^{11}(s')],
\end{equation}
subject to
\begin{align}
(1+\tau_i)c_i^* &= A_i h_i(y_i)l_i^*,\\
\frac{u_{1-l}(c_i^*,1-l_i^*)}{u_c(c_i^*,1-l_i^*)}
&=
\frac{A_i h_i(y_i)}{1+\tau_i},\\
g_i &= \tau_i c_i^*,\\
c_i^* + g_i &= A_i h_i(y_i)l_i^*,
\end{align}
with
\[
\tau_i\ge 0,\qquad g_i\ge 0.
\]

Its continuation value is
\begin{equation}
\mathcal C_i^{11}(s')
=
(1-\lambda_O)(1-\lambda_P)V_i^{11}(s')
+\lambda_O(1-\lambda_P)V_i^{01}(s')
+(1-\lambda_O)\lambda_PV_i^{10}(s')
+\lambda_O\lambda_PV_i^{00}(s').
\end{equation}

\section{Recursive equilibrium}

A recursive Stackelberg equilibrium consists of
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=2em]
    \item state-only regime value functions
    \[
    \{V_O^{00},V_O^{01},V_O^{10},V_O^{11},V_P^{00},V_P^{01},V_P^{10},V_P^{11}\},
    \]
    \item follower conditional objects
    \[
    \widehat V_P(s;a_O),\qquad
    \hat d_P(s;a_O),\qquad
    \hat b_P'(s;a_O),\qquad
    \hat\tau_P(s;a_O),
    \]
    \item leader and sole-borrower policy rules,
    \item household allocations satisfying private optimality,
    \item and bond-price schedules satisfying lenders' Euler equations.
\end{itemize}

Given prices, each sovereign solves the relevant state-action problem above. Given sovereign policies, lenders price sovereign debt according to their stochastic discount factor. The state then evolves according to productivity, debt issuance, default, and readmission.

